 :findings
 ["The prolly-tree is WIDE and SHALLOW: height 2 at 3,000 datoms and 3 at both 15,000 and 60,000, with bottom-level width 10 -> 48 -> 227. Height is logarithmic, width is linear. This is the shape breadth-first parallelism helps -- 訂正4's 'deep and narrow' guess is wrong for the tree."
  "Sync trampoline round trips scale with BLOCK COUNT (11 / 51 / 230); async level-batched waves scale with TREE HEIGHT (2 / 3 / 3). At 60,000 datoms that is 77x fewer sequential fetches and 115x fewer node decodes (26,565 -> 230)."
  "But 91-97% of a hydrate's remaining sequential round trips are NOT the tree. Unfolded novelty is a cons chain ({e, rest}) whose successor CID does not exist until its predecessor is decoded -- width 1, depth = unfolded tx count, prefetch-proof by construction. At the fold threshold (64) the tree costs 2-3 waves and novelty costs 64 hops."
  "The tree's round trips are IDENTICAL across novelty 0 / 20 / 64, which is why 'an empty graph cost the same as a full one' (ADR-2607310900 訂正4) was observed: the cost tracks novelty length, not data size."
  "hot-datoms' async arity routes the snapshot half and the novelty TX-BLOCK reads through the async fetcher, but still enumerates the novelty chain with the SYNC get-fn ((novelty-cids get-fn state)) -- so these hops stay on the trampoline even after the async fix that production already deployed on 2026-07-18."]
 :next
 "Segment the novelty queue the way append-novelty-index! already segments the subject directory (novelty-index-segment-size = 16): 64 hops -> 4. Not: another block-DAG prefetch (the tree is already at 2-3 waves), and not: lowering the fold threshold (that moves the sequential term from reads onto fold, which costs tens of seconds at 60,000 datoms)."
 :caveats
 ["Counts are exact -- the harness runs the REAL pt/scan-prefix under the REAL with-blocks retry algorithm (throw-on-miss sync get-fn, retry from scratch); only the fetch is local."
  "projected-wall-ms is round trips x a SEPARATELY measured R2 RTT. It is a projection, not an end-to-end measurement, and is labelled as such in every cell."
  "In-memory block store on JVM: this measures block-discovery STRUCTURE, not R2 or Worker CPU."
  ":node-decodes covers the SNAPSHOT scan only. with-blocks wraps the whole hydrate, so a real restart also re-walks the state and novelty steps; the true decode total is higher. Round-trip counts are unaffected."
  "The head/state walk (chain.core/head -> state block) is a small fixed number of additional sequential fetches, not counted here -- so the novelty share of the true total is slightly below the figures above."]}
